<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11.png" alt="" width="840" height="446" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-93846" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11.png 1369w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11-300x160.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11-1024x545.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-11-768x408.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Hi!</p>
<p>It feels like I did one of these posts just the other day, but it was a month ago?  Time sure does fly&#8230;</p>
<p>Since there&#8217;s a round number in the title of this blog post, perhaps I should natter on a bit more than usual?  Sure!  If you insist!</p>
<p>So: This is part of a series of posts where I bloviate on how Emacs development is going, partially through gamification of the Emacs bug tracker.  This round, 10% was 205 bugs, so we started at 2050 open bugs, and after closing 10% of those, we&#8217;re now at&#8230;</p>
<p>Drum roll&#8230;</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1.png" alt="" width="840" height="582" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-93823" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1.png 1946w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1-300x208.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1-1024x710.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1-768x532.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-1-1536x1065.png 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>1966 open bugs!  Well, that&#8217;s a decrease, at least &#8212; we had an increase in the previous stretch.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2.png" alt="" width="840" height="610" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-93825" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2.png 1998w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2-300x218.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2-1024x745.png 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2-768x559.png 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-20-2-1536x1117.png 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>But as you can see, I got my act together and did some actual work.</p>
<p>Most of it was just the normal, eh, whatever, but I also went through all the bugs that were tagged as having a patch, and&#8230;</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-14.png"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-14.png" alt="" width="840" height="270" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-93684" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-14.png 881w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-14-300x97.png 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2022-09-14-768x248.png 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>&#8230; applied a bunch of those.  (But a surprising number, perhaps a quarter of those closed, had already actually been applied, but then discussion had just gone on to other things and nobody remembered to close the door after they left.)</p>
<p>As you can see, it&#8217;s been almost a year since the last time somebody took the time to do some triage on the <tt>patch</tt> reports, and that&#8217;s really too long &#8212; I found stuff in there that should have been applied a lot speedier, but&#8230;</p>
<p>Hm&#8230;  I wonder how many bugs I&#8217;ve closed since I started this silly project back in 2019&#8230;</p>
